xnu 0234: the framebuffer probe reads DRAM, over the whole surface

Two ways the probe lied. It read four pixels of the first 64 rows, and a
lock screen is black in that corner, so a frame that drew everything else read
`first 0x00000000 rows 0x0`. And it read before the physical-address clean the
copy to the panel does thirty lines below, while CoreAnimation's mapping of the
same pages is cacheable, so it could report the zeros the CPU had cached before
the GPU wrote. With noblit the RB is measured pointing at the surface itself
(`rb target 0x10c24000 pitch 5760 | surface 2 iova 0x10c24000`) and the probe
still read zero: that is the instrument, not the frame.

Clean first, then sample a grid over the whole surface and print `nonzero
N/M`: `nonzero 0/4600` is an empty surface, `nonzero 3000/4600` is a frame.
The clean moves into one helper both callers use.
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -90,6 +90,41 @@
  */
 static int mondrian_fb_coherent = 1;
 
+/*
+ * Clean and drop the CPU's lines for a surface's pixels, by physical address.
+ * The framebuffer's mapping is Normal Non-Cacheable but CoreAnimation's own
+ * mapping of the same pages is cacheable, so DRAM is not what a plain read of
+ * either sees until this runs. The probe reads through it too: without it the
+ * probe reports what the CPU cached before the GPU wrote, which is how a
+ * correct frame reads as `first 0x00000000`.
+ */
+static void
+mondrian_fb_clean(MondrianSurface *s)
+{
+	uint64_t end, off;
+
+	if (s == NULL || s->memory == NULL || s->bytesPerRow == 0 ||
+	    s->height == 0) {
+		return;
+	}
+	end = (uint64_t)s->offset + (uint64_t)s->bytesPerRow * s->height;
+	off = (uint64_t)s->offset;
+	while (off < end) {
+		IOByteCount length = 0;
+		addr64_t pa = s->memory->getPhysicalSegment(off, &length);
+
+		if (pa == 0 || length == 0) {
+			off += 0x4000;
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (off + length > end) {
+			length = (IOByteCount)(end - off);
+		}
+		(void)mondrian_gpu_uc_flush(pa, length);
+		off += length;
+	}
+}
+
